{
  "format": "owa-conformance-v1",
  "specVersion": "owa.dev/v1",
  "operation": "canonical",
  "notes": [
    "inputJson is parsed as JSON using IEEE-754 binary64 numbers, then canonicalized. This pins reference behavior, not RFC 8785/JCS.",
    "canonicalJson is the exact Unicode string whose UTF-8 bytes are hashed, with no added newline. digest is an independent static SHA-256 golden.",
    "Unpaired surrogate escape vectors pin the existing reference behavior; implementations whose JSON parser forbids them should report that interoperability limitation rather than silently replace characters.",
    "Hard binary64 boundaries (issue #38): the canonical-b64-* vectors pin rule 5 at the edges \u2014 the smallest subnormal and the tokens on either side of its half-way point, the largest subnormal, the smallest normal and its neighbour, the largest finite value and the overflow midpoint (2^53 - 1/2) x 2^971 on both signs, the binary64 neighbours of the 1e-6 and 1e21 layout thresholds, 2^53/2^54/2^60/2^63/2^64, parse ties resolved to the even mantissa, shortest-digit ties resolved to the even final digit (2^50 + 1/4 and 2^50 + 3/4), several decimal tokens for one binary64 value, and the exponent/zero grammar. Expectations were authored with an independent standard-library exact-arithmetic verifier (rational parsing, ties-to-even rounding to binary64, exact rounding-interval shortest-digit search, rule-5 layout, SHA-256), not by either implementation, after it reproduced every pre-existing numeric vector byte for byte.",
    "The fixed/scientific threshold is applied to the shortest decimal being emitted, as the specification's own example 1e-6 -> 0.000001 requires: the binary64 nearest 1e-6 (0x3eb0c6f7a0b5ed8d) is exactly 9.99999999999999954748...e-7, below 10^-6 as a real number, yet its shortest decimal is 1e-6 and it is written in fixed notation. Its lower neighbour (0x3eb0c6f7a0b5ed8c) has shortest decimal 9.999999999999997e-7 and is scientific; 10^21 is exactly representable, so the upper threshold has no such case. Underflow to zero (including negative zero) is finite and canonicalizes as 0; only non-finite results are rejected."
  ],
